{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792923602,"nonce":"V4Ovk0+oMcEmNSsqpqoyj+SZ968=","title":"LSP: caribou-lsp and the caribou lsp command","message":"Part of 1626b90d930d209e848220743e00494408af449d2cdabdddea70f6c0a5337545 (caribou lsp).\n\ncaribou-lsp is a new crate in this workspace. It depends on rayzor-lsp by\ngit revision. The caribou command gains `lsp`, which serves on stdio.\n\n- **Project.** Read the .cbproj (project.rs, cbproj.rs) for:\n  - the source roots and their namespaces;\n  - the declared languages;\n  - the plugins;\n  - the dependencies.\n- **Registry.** Build the project's registry statically, with nothing run:\n  - Wren, Zyntax frontends and plugins as `caribou describe` does;\n  - Haxe from source (see the issue on describing a Haxe module from its\n    source).\n- **Haxe.** Serve it through rayzor-lsp with a provider over the registry,\n  using the one Haxe face. Keep caribou.Bridge.use() out of the editor's\n  session.\n- **Routing.** Send .wren, .lua, .py and frontend files to their analyses,\n  each described in its own issue.\n- **Changes.** When a file changes:\n  - describe its module again;\n  - check again the documents in any language that import it;\n  - publish their diagnostics.\n\n  An edit in a Wren class shows up in the Haxe file that uses it.\n- **Diagnostics.** Use the shape of caribou::diag::Diagnostic, so the\n  structured diagnostics of f570765965bed74b0f2abdb161479905c4ea3330fd9035bb89257f3547d3262d\n  feed the server directly.\n\nDone when the ChimpMonk example (/Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/chimpmonk) opens\nwith no missing-module or unknown-type report in its Haxe, Wren, Lua or\nPython files, and hover on a cross-language use shows the other side's\nsignature.","files":null}]}